@protoman1214 It's not "older" per se, it just developed more conservatively in some ways but not others. There are still many features of modern General American which give it a very different sound from older English pronunciations – notably the cot-caught merger, and the father-bother merger. There is also the phenomenon of flapping which occurs in words like "latter" which makes it homophonous with "ladder" (and neither technically use either a 't' or 'd' sound). Not to mention, most American English dialects have also developed their own shifting variety of vowel sounds, most notably in the South.

What gives people the impression that British English changed a huge amount is the fact that Standard British English no longer has rhoticity. That is really only one thing lost that happens to be particularly noticeable out of many changes that happened to both languages, and it must be noted that the type of rhoticity Americans have today is not the same kind that we had in the past – in the past we used trilled and tapped 'r' sounds, much like Scots still do. In fact, much of British English continued using tapped 'r' until quite recently, and this actually confused American soldiers during the war who perceived it as a 'd' sound because of American English's development of flapping.

Also no, "colour" is the older version, "color" was part of a half-hearted attempt at spelling revision by Webster which he never followed through on. When it comes to spelling British English is always the more conservative version.

@mckendrick7672 of course British commonwealths would lean British. I said internationally… products sold internationally. Of course there is no global agreement to use Americanized English, so technically there is no standard. But the globe can’t agree on anything at all. It’s still the closest thing to an official standard. Airports, international agreements and treaties, etc….

There was lots of turmoil and disagreement even amongst linguists within England throughout history.

The French pronounce and spell color very differently, yet they indeed borrowed it from Latin. I don’t find the pronunciation to be definitive evidence of who it was borrowed from.

@protoman1214 What you're arguing is pointless. We have established that there is no standard, and that the Commonwealth (which is a huge number of countries) and the EU all use British spelling. That alone is a huge portion of the English speakers in the world.

We literally have historic documents from the Anglo-Norman period in which they spelled it as "colour", with the earliest attestation in English being from the Middle English story St Patrick's Purgatory. "Colour" is actually one of the few spellings of the time which survives to this day.

And yes, pronunciation is indicative of from whom it was borrowed. Most words borrowed directly from Latin developed from their original spelling/pronunciation with the English language in line with how English pronunciation as a whole developed. This is especially true for vowel sounds. If English had borrowed it from Latin we'd expect one of the two 'o's to be pronounced as it is in other words, but it isn't. The former vowel is pronounced /ʌ/ which is congruent with how words which developed from an /u/ sound, while the latter is unstressed. Pronunciation tends to change in regular, predictable ways, and based on the fact that many people in older forms of English would also write it as "culur", which is similar to the older French pronunciation *and* the modern French one, pre-standardization there is no reason to believe it was borrowed into English directly from Latin. If it were, we would expect something akin to /kɑlɚ/ and /kɔlə/ or /kəlo:r/ and /kəlɔ:/ rather than /kʌlɚ/ and /kʌlə/ after the Great Vowel Shift.
